---
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro';
import parameters from '../../generated/parameters.json';

const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL.replace(/\/$/, '');
const liveParameters = JSON.stringify(parameters);
---

<Base
  title="What-if tool — Vermont School Budgets"
  description="Try out what merging or closing schools would do to school spending and home tax rates, with every step of the math shown and sourced."
>
  <h1>What-if tool</h1>
  <p class="lede">
    See every step of the math behind a district's student count and tax rate — with a source for
    each one. Change a number and watch every step change with it.
  </p>

  <div class="notice blocking">
    <strong>This tool can't show real Vermont numbers yet — and it won't fake them</strong>
    <p>
      We have not checked any of the legal numbers against the current law yet. So with the live
      settings, every step below tells you what is stopping it instead of showing a number. That is
      the tool doing its job: it would rather stop than hand you a number it can't stand behind.
    </p>
    <p>
      Want to see what a finished walk-through looks like? Switch to the example below. Its numbers
      are made-up round numbers, picked to be easy to follow — and every step says so.
    </p>
  </div>

  <form id="scenario-form">
    <div class="controls">
      <div class="field">
        <label for="parameter-mode">Which numbers to use</label>
        <select id="parameter-mode">
          <option value="live">Live — FY2027 (nothing checked yet)</option>
          <option value="example">Example (not real Vermont law)</option>
        </select>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div id="example-warning" class="notice" hidden>
      <strong>These are not Vermont's real pupil weights</strong>
      <p>
        The numbers in this mode are made-up round numbers — like a high-school weight of exactly
        2, or a low-income weight of exactly 0.5 — picked so you can check the math by hand. They
        show how the math works, not what the real answer is. Every number below is labeled
        "SYNTHETIC TEST FIXTURE — not a real law and not Vermont law," so if you copy a number out
        of this mode, it says what it is.
      </p>
    </div>

    <h2>How many students</h2>
    <p>
      The average number of students, by grade group, for two years. This count is called average
      daily membership. A rule in the law decides how many of those years actually count.
    </p>
    <div class="controls">
      <div class="field">
        <label for="prek-1">FY2025 prekindergarten</label>
        <input id="prek-1" type="number" min="0" step="0.01" value="10" />
      </div>
      <div class="field">
        <label for="k5-1">FY2025 kindergarten through grade 5</label>
        <input id="k5-1" type="number" min="0" step="0.01" value="100" />
      </div>
      <div class="field">
        <label for="g68-1">FY2025 grades 6 through 8</label>
        <input id="g68-1" type="number" min="0" step="0.01" value="50" />
      </div>
      <div class="field">
        <label for="g912-1">FY2025 grades 9 through 12</label>
        <input id="g912-1" type="number" min="0" step="0.01" value="50" />
      </div>
    </div>
    <div class="controls">
      <div class="field">
        <label for="prek-2">FY2026 prekindergarten</label>
        <input id="prek-2" type="number" min="0" step="0.01" value="20" />
      </div>
      <div class="field">
        <label for="k5-2">FY2026 kindergarten through grade 5</label>
        <input id="k5-2" type="number" min="0" step="0.01" value="200" />
      </div>
      <div class="field">
        <label for="g68-2">FY2026 grades 6 through 8</label>
        <input id="g68-2" type="number" min="0" step="0.01" value="100" />
      </div>
      <div class="field">
        <label for="g912-2">FY2026 grades 9 through 12</label>
        <input id="g912-2" type="number" min="0" step="0.01" value="100" />
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="controls">
      <div class="field">
        <label for="state-placed">State-placed students (full-time count, most recent year)</label>
        <input id="state-placed" type="number" min="0" step="0.01" value="0" />
      </div>
      <div class="field">
        <label for="econ">Students in families at or below 185% of the poverty line</label>
        <input id="econ" type="number" min="0" step="0.01" value="40" />
      </div>
      <div class="field">
        <label for="el">Students learning English</label>
        <input id="el" type="number" min="0" step="0.01" value="8" />
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Sparsity and small-school eligibility are derived from density, not
         asserted. 16 V.S.A. 4010(d)(4) sets the density bands and (d)(5)
         gates the small-school weight on 55 or fewer persons per square
         mile, so a checkbox would let the user claim a weight the statute
         would not grant. -->
    <div class="controls">
      <div class="field">
        <label for="density">Persons per square mile in the district</label>
        <input id="density" type="number" min="0" step="0.1" value="30" />
      </div>
      <div class="field">
        <label for="small-school-name">Small school name (optional)</label>
        <input id="small-school-name" type="text" value="" />
      </div>
      <div class="field">
        <label for="small-school-enrollment">That school's average two-year enrollment</label>
        <input id="small-school-enrollment" type="number" min="0" step="0.01" value="" />
      </div>
    </div>

    <h2>Spending and the town</h2>
    <div class="controls">
      <div class="field">
        <label for="spending">Education spending ($)</label>
        <input id="spending" type="number" min="0" step="1000" value="3370000" />
      </div>
      <div class="field">
        <label for="cla">Common level of appraisal (how close town values are to real sale prices)</label>
        <input id="cla" type="number" min="0.1" max="2" step="0.0001" value="0.8" />
      </div>
      <div class="field">
        <!-- Every other field on this form is a figure the user is supposing
             about their own district, so an illustrative default is honest.
             This one is a state-determined statutory input: the statewide
             average district per pupil education spending, increased by
             inflation, that the Secretary of Education determines on or
             before November 15 (32 V.S.A. § 5401(12)). Inventing a number
             here would render a fabricated Vermont figure in the UI, so it
             is left empty. Empty is null, which makes excessSpending
             produce a missing_input node whose blocker tells the user
             exactly which official figure is needed -- a null is
             explicable, an invented number is not. -->
        <label for="statewide-avg">Statewide average spending per student (set by the Secretary of Education)</label>
        <input id="statewide-avg" type="number" min="0" step="1" value="" />
      </div>
    </div>
  </form>

  <h2>Result</h2>
  <div id="summary"></div>
  <div id="blockers"></div>

  <h2>Show the work</h2>
  <p>
    Every step of the math, in order, with what it did and why. This is the tool's own work shown
    directly — not a separate write-up that could drift away from the real math.
  </p>
  <div id="walkthrough"></div>

  <h2>Laws and sources used</h2>
  <div id="citations"></div>

  <h2>What we assumed</h2>
  <p>
    The guesses this what-if starts with, and why each one starts where it does. You can change
    every one. One thing to know: when schools combine, the tool assumes <em>no</em> cuts by
    default. Any cut you see is one you chose and can see — not one the tool made for you.
  </p>
  <div id="assumptions"></div>

  <h2>What this tool will not do</h2>
  <p>
    It does not rank what-ifs, score them, or call any result a "saving." Changes are shown as
    plus-or-minus numbers, both ways. You can set busing costs to go up as well as down, because
    combining schools often makes bus routes longer. A tool that only let that number go down
    would be building in an answer instead of showing the real choices.
  </p>
  <p>
    Some things are left out on purpose and are not part of the math: one-time moving costs,
    building aid, old-debt perks, and exact bus-route planning. If a job is assumed to be combined
    but no salary is given, the tool reports the effect as unknown — not as zero.
  </p>
</Base>

<script define:vars={{ liveParameters }}>
  window.__VT_PARAMETERS__ = JSON.parse(liveParameters);
</script>

<script>
  import { initModelTool } from '../../scripts/model-tool';
  initModelTool(window.__VT_PARAMETERS__ ?? []);
</script>
